     wwhheerreeiiss — locate programs

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     wwhheerreeiiss [--aa] _n_a_m_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The wwhheerreeiiss utility checks the standard binary directories for the speci‐
     fied _n_a_m_e, printing out the paths of any it finds that are executable by
     the user.

     The path searched is:
           /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/usr/X11R6/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/local/sbin

     If the --aa flag is given, wwhheerreeiiss will return a list of all matches in‐
     stead of just the first match.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The wwhheerreeiiss utility exits with one of the following values:

           0       All names were successfully resolved.
           1       Some names were resolved but not all.
           2       No names were resolved.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     apropos(1), find(1), locate(1), which(1)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The wwhheerreeiiss command first appeared in 2BSD, but the original version was
     legally tainted and was not included as part of the 4.4BSD release, such
     that most of the original functionality was lost.

     The --aa option first appeared in OpenBSD 2.3.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     The original version was written by Bill Joy.

